15 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Two-panel data visualization comparing CD4 count over time in HIV patients. Panel A shows original irregular clinic measurements as red points along a dashed line. Panel B depicts preprocessed data on a regular monthly grid with red circles for observed months and blue open circles for imputed months, connected by a blue line labeled \215monthly regularized series.\216 Both y-axes are labeled \215CD4 count \(cells per microliter\),\216 with respective x-axes \215months since ART initiation\216 and \215calendar month index \(one month equals thirty days post-ART\).\216 Panel B includes a legend describing each plotted element.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 34 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Flowchart illustrates a time series transformer architecture for CD4 trajectory prediction, showing input of 12-month CD4 sequence and static patient features, embedding layers, positional encoding, feature fusion, transformer encoder and decoder, and prediction head output.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 35 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Four data visualizations summarize baseline characteristics for 5,436 patients: histogram A shows most have baseline CD4 counts below 350 cells per microliter; bar chart B indicates 3TC+TDF+EFV is the most common initial ART regimen; donut chart C reveals 70.3 percent transmitted HIV via same-sex contact and 27.6 percent heterosexual transmission; histogram D shows age at ART initiation peaks near thirty years, with most patients starting between twenty and fifty.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows a line chart of model training and validation loss in mean squared error \(MSE\) over forty-two epochs, with both losses decreasing steadily. Panel B displays a learning rate schedule as a line chart, which decreases logarithmically over the same epoch range, with four marked points.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Bar chart comparing mean absolute error of seven models on denormalized CD4 scale; Time Series Transformer has the lowest error at 9.7, followed by TFT at 11.2, GRU at 11.9, and LSTM at 12.3, with traditional models performing worse.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Six-panel data visualization showing examples of CD4 count predictions. Panels A to E present line graphs with historical CD4 counts \(blue line\), true post-baseline values \(green circles\), and predicted values \(red squares\) across months. Each graph includes a vertical dashed line at zero months, marking prediction initiation. Panel F displays a scatter plot of true versus predicted CD4 counts with a near-perfect linear fit \(R squared equals 0.9990\), indicating high prediction accuracy.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Panel A shows a histogram of prediction error distribution, centered around zero with a red dashed line at zero. Panel B presents a histogram of absolute error, right-skewed, with a red dashed line indicating mean MAE at 5.36 cells per microliter. Panel C displays a line graph of mean absolute error versus prediction horizon in months, with error bars increasing over time. Panel D shows a Q-Q plot of prediction errors, with blue dots deviating from the red reference line at distribution tails.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Horizontal bar chart titled Feature Importance Analysis ranks variables by relative importance from most to least influential. Baseline CD4 has the highest importance at 1.000, followed by Baseline CD8 \(0.663\), Baseline CD4/CD8 Ratio \(0.593\), and Treatment Regimen \(0.464\). Other variables such as Age, BMI, Days to ART Initiation, Transmission Route, Gender, Weight, Height, and Ethnicity have lower relative importance values, with Ethnicity being the least important at 0.100. Dashed vertical lines indicate thresholds for high and medium importance at 0.7 and 0.5, respectively.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Scatter plot showing baseline CD4 count in cells per microliter versus age in years for various HIV treatment regimens, with colored dots for each regimen and trend lines for grouped regimens.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 55 0 R /Alt (Heatmap showing Pearson correlation coefficients among clinical variables including Baseline CD4, Baseline CD8, Baseline CD4/CD8 Ratio, Age, BMI, Days to ART Initiation, Height, and Weight. Color scale ranges from blue for negative correlations to red for positive correlations. Strongest positive correlations are observed between BMI and Weight at zero point eight seven, and Height and Weight at zero point four eight. Data labels indicate specific correlation values within each cell.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 56 0 R /Alt (Violin plot showing baseline CD4 count distributions in cells per microliter for six initial antiretroviral therapy regimens among 5,426 patients, with each regimen represented by a distinct color and label.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
